[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc1,pc2,pc3
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Client DNS resolver configuration (search/domain suffix) rather than the DNS server itself.

Mechanism: Users are issuing short (unqualified) hostnames like “srv1” and “csw1”. The resolver on the clients does not append a domain search suffix, so the queries are sent as single-label names (“srv1”). The DNS server at 10.5.0.4 (srv1) is configured as an authoritative-only server for jeremysitlab.com with recursion disabled, so it refuses queries outside its zone. This yields “not found/refused” errors for short names, while fully qualified names resolve.

Localization: On all tested clients (pc1, pc2, pc3), /etc/resolv.conf contains only “nameserver 10.5.0.4” with no “search” or “domain” line. Example: pc1 nslookup srv1 10.5.0.4 → “REFUSED”, but getent hosts srv1.jeremysitlab.com → 10.5.0.4. The DNS server (srv1) is up and listening on 10.5.0.4:53 (netstat) and serves the authoritative zone jeremysitlab.com (named.conf, db.jeremysitlab), including a wildcard that resolves any FQDN under that zone.

Root cause: The domain search suffix (jeremysitlab.com) is missing from client resolver configuration (likely lost from DHCP option 15/search list “since this morning”).

Corrective fix: Restore a domain search suffix on clients. Add “search jeremysitlab.com” (or “domain jeremysitlab.com”) to /etc/resolv.conf on pc1/pc2/pc3, or fix DHCP to hand out option 15/119 with “jeremysitlab.com”. After this, short names (e.g., “srv1”) will resolve correctly via srv1 without changing the DNS server. No change to srv1 is required. 
=== END ===